Chamath, well he’s done both. Chamath is regarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:09.420
as the king of SPACs, but this isn’t anything to&nbsp;
be proud of. Everytime you see the word SPAC, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:15.180
can replace it with the word scam, and it wouldn’t&nbsp;
change the meaning all that much. If you’re not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:20.760
familiar with SPACs, it stands for Special Purpose&nbsp;
Acquisiton Company, and it’s really just a scheme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:26.100
to bypass regulation while making deal sponsors&nbsp;
like Chamath even richer. Here’s how it works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:31.440
Let’s say you have an up and coming company that&nbsp;
wants to go public. If you wanted to do it the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:36.240
right way, you would have to go through a bunch&nbsp;
of SEC scrutiny and pay massive fees to simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:41.220
operate as a public company. You’ll have to put&nbsp;
out quarterly reports, host shareholder meetings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:46.200
and probably deal with more audits. While&nbsp;
this is truly quite annoying for businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:52.500
these regulations are what keeps retail investors&nbsp;
atleast somewhat safe. But, what if there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:59.640
way to work around this red tape? Well there is.&nbsp;
Enter SPACs. SPACs are basically shell companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:04.860
that will go public in place of the original&nbsp;
company. People can invest into the SPAC and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:10.140
SPAC will turn around and invest into the original&nbsp;
company. And given that the SPAC is just a shell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:16.260
it’s far easier and cheaper to maintain the SPAC&nbsp;
as a public company. Chamath likes to frame SPACs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:22.020
as some sort of service to retail investors. He’s&nbsp;
apparently leveling the playing field by giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:28.140
everyday people access to private investments. But&nbsp;
really, SPACs are just a way to give the public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:34.620
access to highly volatile private investments that&nbsp;
aren’t quite ready for the public. As such, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:40.440
companies usually get obliterated by the market&nbsp;
once the hype dies down, and that’s exactly what’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:47.640
happened to basically all of Chamath’s SPACs.&nbsp;
Virigin Galactic down 94%. Opendoor down 97%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.360 --> 00:09:00.240
Clover down 95%. SoFi down 85%. Desktop Metal down&nbsp;
96%. Proterra down 88%. Latch down 97%. Sunlight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:08.280
Financial down 94%. Berkshire Grey down 96%.&nbsp;
Basically every single one of Chamath’s SPACs are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:15.000
down 90%. But, none of this matters for Chamath&nbsp;
